hi there i'm back steve kaufman here i'm  back from my  20-day visit to  myanmar  vietnam and korea  and i want to talk about visiting the  visiting the country and learning the  language you often hear that in order to  learn a language you have to visit the  country and some people suggest that you  get a phrase book before going there and  then when you get there you'll be able  to use  these phrases  so i'll talk about my experience in  three countries  uh before going i to uh asia i did buy  uh vietnamese phrasebook with  uh some mp3 some files some audio or cd  which i listened to  i don't know how much time i put in a  couple of hours  um  and uh  but i went to myanmar first so  before uh i didn't do anything before  but when i went there i bought a little  book burmese okay  my experience is as follows  uh in burma for six days  seven days  i must have asked people  20 times how do you say hello how do you  say goodbye how do you say thank you  maybe please i mean those are really the  only words that you have a chance  of being able to say if you're there for  six days  i came out of it with  maybe two that i can remember mingalabar  which is  hello good day whatever  and um  jesuba i think jesus  which is thank you and tata is goodbye  that's all and i had to ask literally  over and over again i kept on forgetting  the only one that sticks after a while  is mingalabar because it's  it's  people say it to you all the time  because they're so friendly and i had to  have a little thing in my mind like ming  byla like in chinese to try and remember  mingalabar the point is  it's very difficult to pick up phrases  and looking in this phrase book  i mean it was hopeless to me how do you  say you know where is the or  not a chance unless the language is  pretty close to a language that you  already speak  so um stimulating uh to be there  but in terms of actually trying to say  something and and if they if even if i  were to say something so well like hello  that they started to speak to me in  their local language i would be lost so  looking at a few phrases before you go  there in the case of manbar useless  vietnam despite the fact that i studied  and i studied but i did listen and try  to get something again i ended up with  four or five words sincha which means  hello  uh  come on which means thank you  tampered which means goodbye  sin loy which means excuse me and i  think that's about it and that's after  seven days lots of effort i even bought  a dictionary so that i could perhaps  look up the word that i wanted to say  useless  uh i even also went and bought this book  to see how  the uh chinese characters are pronounced  in vietnamese i found that there aren't  nearly as many of those words in  vietnamese as there are in korean or  in japanese so the chinese characters  aren't that big of a help  so all i'm saying is going there  with the phrase book hoping to be able  to have any kind of meaningful  communication is a hopeless dream  all you can do it's not communication  it's about saying something in their  language which  it's like showing off and very often  they appreciate it so if you say  come on thank you in vietnamese people  appreciate it more than if you just said  thank you sometimes so it's not a major  way of learning the language uh korean  of course i've spent a lot of time in  korean find korean difficult despite all  the time that i spent on korean when i  first arrived in korea actually it was  difficult to swing into action right  away to try to use the language i went  to the subway  and there's a couldn't quite figure out  how to use their vending machine and i  asked one of the employees of the metro  system and he couldn't really understand  what i was saying i couldn't understand  what he was saying communication  zero  however  within two or three days of being there  of using it a little more here a little  more there all of my passive learning  got activated quite quickly and in our  meetup night where we had both koreans  and foreigners who spoke korean i was  able to speak quite a bit of korean and  then subsequently when i had to talk to  taxi drivers i had other situations  where i was dealing with people who  couldn't speak english  you know it was surprising how after a  day or two or three  the passive learning gets activated so i  sort of want to restate my conviction  that if you want to go and benefit from  visiting the country make sure that you  put in enough time we're talking six  months significant investment in the  language and even then when you get  there there will be a day or two or  three before you can activate it but  then  it's not just that you use it and hear  it more there's something about being in  an environment where that's the language  of communication so that it's no longer  just an academic exercise that seems to  that sort of atmospheric that  environment has the effect of of  activating your language so when you  have put enough time in your language by  all means visit the country it'll do  wonders for your language if you haven't  put enough  effort into it ahead of time  visiting the country it's still a  pleasant thing to do but you won't be  able to communicate and certainly  certainly you won't be able to rely on a  phrasebook so i went a bit longer than  my usual  three four minutes uh we'll see if we  can get a transcript done for this as  well thank you for listening bye for now
 

